For receiving photos from iPhone using the “Send Batch to Server” iOS share-sheet shortcut.
Build it with ./build_shortcut.sh (unqforge, installed into .venv if needed). The builder
embeds PHOTO_TOKEN from the environment, or ~/.config/photo_token, in the generated
shortcut and uses Authorization: Bearer on both POST /wanted and PUT uploads. Source
files contain no secret; generated files in shortcut_downloads/ are ignored by Git.

The shortcut hashes each shared original once, sends all hashes in one JSON POST /wanted,
parses the wanted array, then PUTs only wanted originals. Cached hashes stay aligned with
the original input order. If nothing is wanted, the upload loop is skipped. The receiver supplies an
extension from file content. iphone_shortcut_prompt.txt describes the same workflow.

Download page: https://photos.example.com:9443/iphone_photos/shortcuts/
Served by photos-receiver.service, with the photo browser's existing login at the HTTPS
proxy. The proxy sets X-Photo-Shortcut-User only after successful authentication and strips
it from other receiver requests; the loopback-only receiver trusts that identity header.
No separate download server is needed. ./build_shortcut.sh refreshes the downloads without
restarting the receiver. Pages and artifacts use Cache-Control: no-store during iteration.
Generated files include the token and are ignored by Git. They require Apple signing:
the page explains unqforge web signing (interactive human check; signing service receives
the embedded token) or local Mac signing.
The pre-existing root-level Send Batch to Server.shortcut is the older Cherri output.

Photos and their SQLite metadata are stored in /var/lib/photos.
Run ./run.sh to receive photos and ./browse.sh to browse them on port 8095
(PHOTO_DIR=/other/library ./browse.sh browses a different library).

Browser (FastAPI + htmx + daisyUI, templates/; daisyUI 5.7.50, Tailwind browser 4.3.3 and
htmx 2.0.11 are vendored in static/vendor/, so no CDN/internet is needed): grid of photos, ARW raws and MP4 videos with
infinite scroll, a viewer with prev/next (arrow keys), two notes fields per item (autosaved;
#hashtags in either become tags), a #Tags page, and search. Search terms match notes, filename,
camera, lens and date (e.g. 2026-11); #tag terms filter by tag; terms are ANDed.

Bulk hashtags: in any photo grid (Library, search/tag results, or an album), press Select,
pick photos and use Add hashtags… / Remove hashtags…. Enter tags with or without #,
separated by spaces or commas. Applied tags are stored in photos.hashtags, separately
from note1/note2; photo_tags indexes the union for search, badges, and the #Tags page.
Saving notes preserves applied tags. Removing a hashtag clears it from photos.hashtags
and removes every matching hashtag token in both notes (case-insensitive), preserving
all other text and longer/different tags. Album/folder descriptions are not changed.
The database field is added automatically when each library is next opened.

Albums (iCloud Photos style): the Albums page is one tree of folders and albums. Drag the grip
handle to reorder or to move items into another (open) folder; create folders/albums at the top or
inside a folder from each row; click an album to see its photos. Folders nest; albums reference
photos (a photo can be in several albums; removing it or deleting an album never deletes the photo;
deleting a folder deletes what's inside it). To add photos: tick the checkbox that appears on a
photo (shift-click for a range) or press Select, then "Add to album…" - the picker searches as you
type, shows each album's folder path, and can create a new album. The viewer shows the album
breadcrumb and which albums a photo is in. Albums and folders have a description whose #hashtags
appear on the #Tags page. Stored in inbox.db: collections (with manual position), album_photos,
collection_tags. Drag & drop in the albums tree is plain pointer-event code in templates/albums.html.

Metadata: on upload the receiver runs exiftool (all tags incl. Sony MakerNotes) into
photos.exif (JSON) plus taken_at/camera/lens/width/height/duration columns. Sony clip sidecars
(C0001M01.XML) are uploaded by send_camera.py and stored in sidecar_xml + SonyXML:* keys.
Without exiftool a basic Pillow EXIF extraction is used. Needs system packages:
    sudo apt install libimage-exiftool-perl ffmpeg     # ffmpeg = video thumbnails
then ./.venv/bin/python refresh_metadata.py to upgrade existing rows (--all redoes everything).

Sony ZV-E10 via a laptop: copy send_camera.py (stdlib only, Python 3.10+) and the CA cert
/etc/ssl/nucbox3/nucbox3-ca/nucbox3.crt to the laptop. On the camera set Menu > Setup > USB >
USB Connection Mode = "Mass Storage", plug it into the laptop, open it in the file manager, then:
    python3 send_camera.py --cacert nucbox3.crt            # auto-detects the card
    python3 send_camera.py /media/me/CARD --dry-run --cacert nucbox3.crt
It hashes JPG/ARW/MP4 on the card, asks the server which are new (/wanted, same as the iPhone
shortcut) and PUTs only those straight from the card - nothing is copied to the laptop.
--server / $PHOTO_SERVER overrides the URL (e.g. https://192.0.2.10:9443/iphone_photos on LAN).
send_camera.sh and ingest_camera.sh use nucbox3.crt next to them (or $PHOTO_SERVER_CACERT) and
always verify the server. They no longer fall back to --insecure when the certificate file is
missing - that sent the upload token to an unverified server; pass --insecure yourself if you
really want that.

Canon PowerShot SX540 HS (and other PTP USB cameras): install gphoto2 on the laptop
(e.g. sudo apt install gphoto2 on Debian/Ubuntu), connect using a Mini-B USB data cable,
and turn the camera on. Close Dolphin/Gwenview camera windows to release the USB device.
Use the updated send_camera.py and send_camera.sh on the laptop, then run:
    ./send_camera.sh --gphoto --dry-run      # read checksums and report what's new
    ./send_camera.sh --gphoto                # upload new items
With no mounted camera card, ./send_camera.sh also tries gphoto2 automatically.
If several USB cameras are attached, --camera-port usb:001,041 selects one (the port
changes on reconnection; gphoto2 --auto-detect lists current ports). A Dolphin camera:/
address is not a filesystem path; use --gphoto rather than passing that address.
The uploader reads all camera media to calculate hashes each run, with at most one
media file temporarily spooled to the laptop at a time. Wanted media is read again for
upload; temporary files are removed on completion, errors, or Ctrl+C. Camera files are
never deleted. Mounted cards are read directly and hashed afresh on every run. Persisted file timestamps
are not trusted as proof that the original bytes are unchanged. Repeated scans therefore
read the card again; this deliberately prioritizes correct duplicate decisions.
No server restart or changes are required for this client feature.

Sidecars: malformed XML capture dates fall back to other metadata or the file time rather
than blocking a valid original. A local dry run reports missing-sidecar repairs without
writing them. If a clip uploads but its XML does not, the next send_camera.py run sends just the XML
(it tells /wanted which hashes it has a sidecar for; the server answers with sidecar_wanted).

Upload token (off by default): put PHOTO_TOKEN=<secret> in /etc/photos-receiver.env (mode 600) and
restart photos-receiver; every request must then carry "Authorization: Bearer <secret>" (or an
X-Photo-Token header). send_camera.py reads it from --token, $PHOTO_TOKEN or ~/.config/photo_token;
the iPhone shortcut needs the header added to both of its requests.

Services: systemd/photos-receiver.service and systemd/photos-browser.service (installed in
/etc/systemd/system, run as photos). While /encrypted is locked they fail to start and retry every 15 s,
so they come up on their own once it is unlocked.

Browser address: https://<server>:9443/photos/ (Caddy basic auth; logins in /etc/caddy/photos-users).
The browser itself listens only on 127.0.0.1:8095 and builds every URL from X-Forwarded-Prefix,
so it also works unprefixed when run by hand. Python packages are in requirements.txt; run.sh and
browse.sh install them into .venv (venv.sh).

Deleting: "Delete from library" in the viewer, or Delete in the selection bar. The row (with its
notes, tags and album places) is removed, the file moves to <library>/.trash, and the hash is kept
in deleted_photos so the phone and camera syncs do not upload it again. If a file cannot be moved
to the trash (e.g. a read-only disk) that photo is kept and the browser says so. restore_deleted.py
remains available to list the trash and put items back by SHA-256 prefix - with their notes, hashtags, Sony sidecar,
album places and chosen album covers (albums deleted meanwhile are skipped; a newer cover
choice is kept; photos deleted before this was recorded come
back bare). It never overwrites an existing file. The trash is only emptied on request:
    ./.venv/bin/python restore_deleted.py --purge 30    # files deleted 30+ days ago; 0 = everything
Purged photos cannot be restored, but their hashes stay remembered, so they are still not re-uploaded.
The browser's Trash page provides the same restore and permanent-delete operations on selected
items. "Available to restore" shows files still in the trash; "All deleted history" also shows
purged/missing originals with restore disabled. Recovery never overwrites an existing file.
The browser and CLI share photo_trash.py, including metadata, tags, album membership and
cover restoration. Restore can warn that the photo also exists in another library.

send_camera.py names files from the recorded capture time (the clip's sidecar XML, else EXIF), not
the card's file timestamps, which a laptop can misread by hours.

Libraries: photos live in separate, self-contained libraries that can sit on different disks
(USB, SSD, the encrypted dataset). A library is a folder with the files, inbox.db and a marker,
library.json (permanent id + name), so it is recognised wherever its disk is mounted. The server
remembers every library it has seen in ~/.config/photos/libraries.json; one whose marker cannot be
read (unplugged, or a locked dataset) is offline and nothing is created in its place. Looked for
in: remembered paths, /media/<user>/*, /run/media/<user>/*, /media/*, /mnt/* (the volume and its
top-level folders), plus $PHOTO_LIBRARY_ROOTS.
    ./libraries.sh                               # list: online/offline, item counts, upload target
    ./libraries.sh create /media/me/DISK/photos "Sony 2026"
    ./libraries.sh inbox "Sony 2026"             # where the iPhone shortcut uploads (no restart needed)
    ./libraries.sh duplicates allow|refuse       # photos that another library already has
    ./libraries.sh rename NAME|ID NEWNAME ; ./libraries.sh forget NAME|ID
Browser: every page is under /lib/<id>/; the navbar has a library switcher and /libraries lists
them all. Albums, tags, notes and the trash are per library.
A remote or unplugged library has a "View catalogue" action on /libraries. The read-only
page lists cached originals and searches filename, camera, lens, date and SHA-256; terms are
ANDed and media type can be filtered. It includes a last-report time and an incomplete-sync
warning if reported and cached counts differ. Files/previews/notes/tags/albums stay on the
original disk. When that disk returns, the catalogue URL redirects to its live photo grid.
Offline item counts now agree across the browser, libraries.sh and GET /libraries when a
cached count is available; offline catalogues reflect what was last reported, not a live disk.
Camera: send_camera.py shows a menu of the plugged-in libraries (--library NAME skips it; without a
terminal it uses the upload library). A file that another library already has - plugged in, or
unplugged but seen before (see catalogue.db below) - is
skipped with a warning and a count; --allow-duplicates uploads it anyway.
refresh_metadata.py and restore_deleted.py take --library NAME (default: the upload library).
The photo libraries are deliberately not part of the gmktec server backup: every library holds a
.photos-receiver-library file, and that backup skips any folder containing it (tar
--exclude-tag-all), wherever the library is.

Laptop ingestion (camera -> a library on the laptop; the server is only told about it):
ingest_camera.sh copies from a USB camera into a library that lives on the laptop or on a
USB drive plugged into it. Unlike send_camera.py it needs this whole repository on the laptop
(./ingest_camera.sh builds .venv from requirements.txt) and, for full metadata, exiftool.
Linux (macOS should work; Windows is not supported - use send_camera.py there).
    ./libraries.sh create ~/photos/trip "Trip 2026"     # once; or a folder on a USB drive
    ./ingest_camera.sh                    # mounted card, else a gphoto2 camera; asks which library
    ./ingest_camera.sh --gphoto --library "Trip 2026"
    ./ingest_camera.sh --dry-run          # what is new; copies nothing
    ./ingest_camera.sh sync               # only send library info to the server
Files are named, recorded in the library's inbox.db and indexed exactly as an upload through
the receiver would be (photo_store.py is shared by both), the copy is checked against the
card, and nothing is deleted from the camera. A gphoto2 camera's files are each downloaded
once, straight onto the library's disk. Mounted-card ingestion refuses to start if the new originals will not fit;
PTP checks available space as files are read. FAT32 drives cannot take files of 4 GB or more (format the drive as exFAT or ext4).
Mounted-card dry runs now validate batch capacity and FAT32 limits too. PTP must read each
file to identify it: it checks capacity per download, forecasts cumulative new imports in
dry runs, and retains completed originals if a later read/copy fails. Partial imports are
reported and retries resume without duplicate copies. A verification mismatch invalidates
the cached source hash so retry can re-read a same-size/mtime source that changed.
Registered libraries must still have the matching marker before any database/file write;
an unplugged disk is never replaced by a new database under its old mount point. Clean,
already-initialized read-only libraries can be viewed, while ingestion requires writable
storage. Crash-left temporary parts are swept after a day, as before.
No network is needed. When the server is reachable (same --server / $PHOTO_SERVER, CA cert
and token as send_camera.py) the laptop then reports every library it has: name, hostname,
path, item count and a catalogue of the photos (hash, file names, size, capture time, camera,
lens) plus the hashes deleted from it. If the server cannot be reached the ingest still
succeeds; the next ingest or "./ingest_camera.sh sync" sends it. The sync compares one
checksum first, so a run with nothing new is a single request. Changed snapshots are staged
on the receiver; original published rows, deleted hashes, counts and last-success time stay
unchanged while batches arrive. A verified full checksum is required before publication.
New clients confirm the commit explicitly. The previous client request sequence also
publishes atomically; upgrade the receiver before deploying the new laptop client. Automatic laptop sync can be enabled with:
    ./.venv/bin/python configure_laptop_sync.py --cacert ./nucbox3.crt
The installer writes this user's systemd service/timer with the checkout's actual path,
keeps credentials in the existing ~/.config/photo_token, and retries every minute while
the user session is running. Catalogue-affecting edits request a nonblocking sync straight
away. This timer is now installed on laptop-host. Other machines remain opt-in.
/libraries shows Pending / Syncing / Failed / Synced, errors and the last successful sync,
with a Sync libraries now button. Changes made during a sync stay pending until a later
snapshot succeeds. Failed/offline attempts never erase the previous success time.
--no-sync makes no network requests and does not schedule a background sync itself.
An independently installed timer keeps running; use configure_laptop_sync.py --disable
when you want automatic sync switched off too.
On the server such a library shows on /libraries and in ./libraries.sh as "on <laptop>" with
its item count and last sync. The files are NOT on the server: its photo grid cannot be opened there, and it cannot
be an upload target or be made the upload library. Its read-only catalogue can be viewed. To browse it on the laptop run
./browse.sh --host 127.0.0.1 (the browser has no login of its own and listens on every network
interface by default; on the server Caddy provides the password), or plug its drive into the
server - it is then an ordinary library. To keep the laptop's browser running (and started at
boot), install systemd/photos-laptop-browser.service as a user unit; it is installed on
laptop-host. The first ./ingest_camera.sh or ./browse.sh run
on a laptop needs internet once, to install the Python packages into .venv.
A drive formatted ext4 keeps file owners: use the same user id on both machines (or exFAT),
or the server cannot read what the laptop wrote.
Not sent to the server: the photos themselves, notes, hashtags, albums, the trash.

Where things are stored: each library has its own SQLite database, <library>/inbox.db. The
server has no database of libraries - ~/.config/photos/libraries.json lists every library
seen (name, last path, last seen, and for a laptop library what the laptop reported). The one
server-wide SQLite file is ~/.config/photos/catalogue.db (photo_catalogue.py): for every
library, what it held when last seen - one row per photo (hash, names, size, capture time,
camera, lens) and its deleted hashes. The server's own libraries are recorded while plugged in
(when found, hourly, and on each upload, delete and restore); laptop libraries by their sync.
It exists so the duplicate check still works for a library that is away. It is a cache -
delete it and it is rebuilt - and a library that is plugged in is always answered from its
own inbox.db. If an unplugged disk was changed on another machine meanwhile, the server only
learns of it when the disk is plugged in again (or that machine syncs).

Duplicates across libraries: the "already in another library" check covers plugged-in
libraries, the server's own unplugged ones (named "X (not plugged in)") and libraries on a
synced laptop, so the server refuses (./libraries.sh duplicates refuse, the
default) an iPhone or send_camera.py upload of something a laptop library holds, naming it
"Trip 2026 (on <laptop>)". ingest_camera.sh asks the server the same question before
copying and skips what any server library has (--allow-duplicates overrides; the laptop's
own ./libraries.sh duplicates setting is separate from the server's). Offline it can only
check the laptop's own libraries; overlaps are then reported at the next sync, and nothing
is removed. A photo deleted from a server library is still ingested on the laptop, with a note.
Library names are only checked per machine: if a laptop library has the same name as a
server one, the sync warns - rename one (./libraries.sh rename; the next sync carries it over).
Do not copy a library folder to a second machine and keep using both: the two copies share
an id and are not merged. The server refuses a sync for a library it has plugged in itself.
Deploying: restart photos-receiver on the server once (new /remote/... and /known requests).

Dates: photos are ordered and searched by capture time; one without a capture time uses its
arrival time (UTC) in the same form, so it sorts in the right place to within the UTC offset.
UTC-only video dates (QuickTime CreateDate/MediaCreateDate) use the capture timezone saved
in that photo's metadata at first indexing. The timezone travels in inbox.db, so metadata
refresh or restore on another machine does not move the date. Camera-local EXIF/Sony sidecar
dates keep their recorded wall time. For older indexed clips the existing taken_at and UTC
EXIF are used to recover the original capture offset, including during restore; a clip with
no previous capture time uses the timezone of its first indexing. This fixes the previous
machine-timezone limitation without re-indexing existing real libraries automatically.

Uploads are never stored under the names of a library's own files (inbox.db*, library.json,
anything hidden): such a name gets an "upload_" prefix. Temporary .part_* files left by an
upload or ingest that died are removed after a day.

Validation: install test dependencies with ./.venv/bin/pip install -r requirements-test.txt,
then run ./.venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -v. Tests use temporary libraries
and registries; they do not modify the machine's photo libraries. See VALIDATION.md for
verified coverage and the accepted hardware assumptions. CONCEPTUAL_PARITY.md records the
conceptual review, new browser workflows and remaining scope boundaries.

Command-line ingestion re-audit: both destination paths hash source contents afresh each
run. send_camera.py supplies X-Photo-SHA256 so an updated receiver can reject bytes that
changed after the hash pass before recording a file. A missing/wrong-sized original with
an existing database row is offered for repair rather than silently counted as present;
repair preserves notes, tags, albums, cover choices, arrival time and source metadata.
A wrong-sized existing file is retained under its old name while the recovered original
gets a non-colliding name. Deliberately deleted hashes remain excluded. Duplicate-policy
messages reflect what the receiver actually requested, and dry runs distinguish originals
to repair from new imports. Imports themselves check only that an existing original is present with the right size;
same-sized damage is found by the integrity check below.

Integrity across disks and machines
-----------------------------------
    ./libraries.sh verify NAME|ID            re-read every original against its SHA-256
    ./libraries.sh verify NAME|ID --repair   also quarantine and recover
    ./libraries.sh acknowledge NAME|ID       clear a library's warnings

verify reads every original again and also checks the database, the trash and files in the
folder that no record points to. --repair never deletes: a damaged original moves to the
library's .quarantine folder and is replaced from a good copy (a stray file in the same
library, or the same photo in another library plugged in to this machine, re-hashed as it
is copied). What cannot be recovered stays recorded with its notes, tags and albums; the
next import of that photo from the camera or phone puts it back. A read-only library can be
verified but not repaired.

Every library carries a change counter. Each machine remembers the counter and the contents
it last recorded, so the Libraries page, `libraries.sh list` and the laptop's sync report:
- an older copy or restored backup (counter went backwards);
- photos that vanished without being deleted through the application;
- two copies of one library plugged in at once (only the one changed most recently is used);
- the result of the last verify, while problems remain.
Warnings stay until acknowledged. Copies are reported, never merged or written to.

A library that comes back as an older copy, or without photos it had, becomes read-only on
that machine: it can be viewed, verified and synced, but uploads, camera ingests, browser
edits and repairs are refused with the reason. It is writable again once you acknowledge it
(./libraries.sh acknowledge, or the button on the Libraries page), or by itself when the
up-to-date copy is plugged in. Other libraries are unaffected.

A change the server refuses or never receives (read-only library, disk pulled, server down)
shows a "Not saved" message on the page. A page load for a library that has gone goes to the
Libraries page, which says why. Dates in ./libraries.sh and restore_deleted.py are local,
as in the browser.

An original that verify found missing or damaged and could not recover no longer counts as
"already in another library" once that disk is unplugged, so the photo can be imported
elsewhere; it counts again once repaired. (This is recorded on the machine that ran verify;
it is not sent to the server for a laptop library.) Restoring a deleted photo returns it
only to the albums it was in, never to a newer album that was given a deleted album's number.
A '#tag' search matches tags as they are stored, and a plain word also finds tags applied
from the grid.

Stored originals are flushed to disk before they are recorded, on uploads as well as camera
ingests. The duplicate check answers from the catalogue when a listed disk cannot be read
(pulled a moment ago). A change whose catalogue update was lost is caught up at the next scan.
See CLI_REAUDIT.md for the latest live fake-USB checks and remaining test boundaries.
